diagnosis_text:
The reported inter-department routing fault between IT (192.168.1.0/24) and CS (192.168.2.0/24) is **not present** in the live network. A comprehensive multi-phase investigation found no anomaly at any layer.

**Affected component:** None — all subsystems are healthy.

**Mechanism (why the ticket is unconfirmed):** Router0 has both department interfaces fully operational: eth0 = 192.168.1.1/24 (IT LAN, UP, 0 errors) and eth1 = 192.168.2.1/24 (CS LAN, UP, 0 errors). Its FRR/RIP daemon is running and has redistributed both directly-connected prefixes as well as WAN routes to router1 and router2. A live end-to-end ping from it_hod (192.168.1.2) directly to 192.168.2.2 (a CS segment host) returned 3/3 packets with 0% loss and ~1.3 ms RTT. The gateway 192.168.1.1 is also reachable from it_hod with 0% loss.

**Localization:** Router0 eth0/eth1 are UP; no interface was down, detached, or flapping. No flap script or process was found on router0. Kernel carrier counters show zero carrier changes. No journalctl link-down events exist.

**Root cause:** No fault is present. The inter-department path (IT → router0 → CS) is fully operational at L2, L3, and routing-control-plane levels with no traffic-shaping or ACL interference.

**Fix:** No corrective action is needed. If the user experienced a transient disruption earlier, it left no mechanism or historical trace in the current lab state — the claim cannot be confirmed as a reproducible fault.
